1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,480
Project Meridian is 10% over budget. Should the agent send a red alert?

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:12,500
The obvious answer is yes. It may also be wrong. The project has an $80M budget.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:18,340
Validated EAC is $88M: $8M of exposure. A fixed 5% rule sees only one thing.

4
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:22,940
Ten is greater than five. But now look at the decision context.

5
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:28,140
If $8.5M of recognized contingency remains, the variance appears for the first time…

6
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:33,460
…and 12 months remain: MONITOR. If only $1M remains and the variance persists…

7
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:38,120
…for three cycles, with two months left and contractual exposure: ESCALATE.

8
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:43,380
Same project. Same 10%. Completely different interruption.

9
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:46,380
Evaluate residual exposure, persistence, time to act and consequence.

10
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:51,220
Deterministic rules decide MONITOR, REVIEW or ESCALATE. The LLM explains why.

11
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:56,940
The threshold finds a candidate signal. Context decides whether it deserves attention.
